-- title:
--   Theorem 1(i) — the optimal hedge $\theta^*=-\xi+\iota$ (corrected: $\mathbb P^M\ne\mathbb P$)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds: $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12).
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 4–5 hold, $Z_T$ is not almost surely equal to $1$, and $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$. Fix a target $m$ and a decision $(P,R)$; let $(\xi,\delta)$ be any integrands representing $V(P,R)$ as in (18), and $\zeta$ any $\mathcal G$-adapted integrand with $Z^M_t=Z^M_0+\int_0^t\zeta\,dX$ (16). Put
--   $$
--   \lambda_m=\frac{mZ^M_0-V_0(P,R)}{Z^M_0-1}.
--   $$
--   Then the hedging problem (14) has an optimal solution $(\theta^*,\chi^*)\in\mathcal A_X$, $\chi^*_t=\int_0^t\theta^*_s\,dX_s$, with
--   $$
--   \theta^*_t(P,R)=-\xi_t(P,R)+\iota_t(P,R),\qquad \iota_t(P,R)=-\frac{\zeta_t}{Z^M_t}\Big[\lambda_m-V_t(P,R)-\chi^*_t\Big]\qquad (20)
--   $$
--   for almost every $t\in[0,T]$, almost surely. Precisely: it meets the constraint $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)+\chi^*_T]=m$; its variance equals $B(m,P,R)$; and every admissible strategy that meets the constraint and attains $B(m,P,R)$ has the same terminal gains $\chi^*_T$ almost surely.
--
--   The first term of $\theta^*$ is the risk-mitigation position, the second the investment position.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$; reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$: $\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\sigma(Y_s)]$ a.s. for $s\le t\le T$ and bounded measurable $g$; reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$ → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained only for $t\le T$; reason: Mathlib's Brownian motion lives on $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$; values after $T$ never enter
--   - encoding: stochastic integrals $\int\theta\,dX$, $\int b\,d\tilde B$, $\int\eta\,dB$ with almost-sure integrability → the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` (integrand cut off after $T$, square integrable on every path) plus a pathwise $du$-integral of the drift on every path; reason: under the augmented filtration an integrand can be set to zero on the exceptional null set without losing measurability, so the classes agree up to indistinguishability
--   - reading: for $u\le v$, $C_v-C_u$ is adapted to the σ-algebra generated by $\{X_t,u\le t\le v\}$ (p.11) → $C_v-C_u$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma\{X_t:u\le t\le v\}$, for $u\le v\le T$; reason: "adapted" for a single random variable means measurable
--   - encoding: $Z_t=e^{-\int_0^t\eta_s dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds}$ (12) → $Z_t=\exp(-I_t-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_u^2du)$ with $I$ a $\mathcal G$-adapted version of the Itô integral $\int\eta\,dB$; reason: an Itô integral is fixed only up to a null set; the paper uses that $Z$ is $\mathcal G$-adapted (p.ec23)
--   - encoding: "martingale" (relative to $\mathcal F_t$, footnote 4) → `IsMartingaleOn`: adapted, integrable, and the martingale identity for $s\le t\le T$; reason: the paper's martingales live on $[0,T]$; Mathlib's `Martingale` constrains all times
--   - encoding: "adapted" integrands $a,b,\gamma,\xi,\delta,\zeta$ → progressively measurable integrands; reason: the standard measurability of a stochastic integrand; on an augmented filtration an adapted process has a progressive modification
--   - correction: Theorem 1 assumes only Assumptions 4–5 and asserts $Z^M_0=\mathbb E(Z_T^2)>1$ → the added hypothesis that $Z_T$ is not almost surely $1$ (i.e. $\mathbb P^M\ne\mathbb P$); reason: counterexample: $\mu\equiv0$, $\sigma\equiv1$, $C\equiv0$ satisfy Assumptions 4–5 with $Z\equiv1$, so $Z^M_0=1$, "$>1$" fails and $\lambda_m$ and (21) divide by zero; with $Z_T\ne1$, Jensen gives $\mathbb E Z_T^2>1$
--   - encoding: the mean–variance problems (7), (13), (14) presuppose $\mathrm{Var}[H_T(P,R)]<\infty$ (p.9, p.ec23) → the hypothesis $\mathbb E[((C_T)^-)^2]<\infty$ (`NegPartL2`); reason: with the Gaussian term $\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$ it is equivalent to $H_T(P,R)\in L^2(\mathbb P)$ for all $(P,R)$; it excludes only the case where problem (14) is undefined
--   - encoding: $B(m,P,R)$ the minimum of a variance over admissible hedges with mean $m$ (14) → an infimum in $[0,\infty]$ of the extended variance `evariance`; reason: an empty feasible set gives $+\infty$, not a junk $0$; attainment is part of Theorem 1
--   - reading: "The optimal solution to the problem in (14) is" (20), a feedback formula in $\theta^*$ through $\int_0^t\theta^*dX$ → existence of an admissible pair $(\theta^*,\chi^*)$ satisfying (20) $dt\otimes d\mathbb P$-a.e., meeting the constraint and attaining $B$, plus uniqueness of the optimal terminal gains; reason: the fixed-point formula is stated as a property of the pair; "the" optimal solution is read as unique (in terminal wealth)
--   - reading: $\xi_t$ and $\zeta_t$ "defined in (16) and (18)" → every representing pair $(\xi,\delta)$ of (18) and every representing $\zeta$ of (16); reason: the stronger reading
--   - encoding: an admissible strategy $\vartheta=\{\theta_t\}$ with $\chi_t(\vartheta)=\int_0^t\theta_s dX_s$ (EC.7.15) → a pair $(\theta,\chi)$ with $\chi$ an $\mathcal F$-adapted version of the gains on $[0,T]$; reason: the stochastic integral is fixed only up to null sets, so the strategy is carried with its gains; the terminal gains are the same random variable a.s.
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Theorem 1(i), (20), p.15; proof §EC.7.3, pp.ec27–ec43

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Hedging

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV

/-- Theorem 1(i), arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.15 (corrected: `P^M ≠ P`): suppose Assumptions 4–5
hold and `Z_T` is not almost surely equal to `1`. The optimal solution of the hedging problem
(14) is
`θ*_t(P,R) = −ξ_t(P,R) + ι_t(P,R)` (20), where
`ι_t(P,R) = −(ζ_t/Z^M_t)[λ_m − V_t(P,R) − ∫_0^t θ*_s(P,R) dX_s]`,
`λ_m = (mZ^M_0 − V_0(P,R))/(Z^M_0 − 1)`, with `Z^M_t`, `ζ_t` of (16) and `ξ_t` of (18).

Precisely: for every `m`, `(P, R)`, every representation (18) of `V(P,R)` with integrands
`(ξ, δ)` and every `G`-adapted `ζ` with `Z^M_t = Z^M_0 + ∫_0^t ζ dX`, there is an admissible
`(θ*, χ*) ∈ A_X` such that, for almost every `t ∈ [0, T]`, almost surely,
`θ*_t = −ξ_t − (ζ_t/Z^M_t)(λ_m − V_t − χ*_t)`; it meets the mean constraint
`E[H_T(P,R) + χ*_T] = m`; it attains the optimal value `B(m,P,R)`; and every admissible strategy
that meets the constraint and attains `B(m,P,R)` has the same terminal gains `χ*_T`.

**Formalization Note** The feedback definition of `θ*` through its own gains
`χ*_t = ∫_0^t θ* dX` is stated as a property of the pair. The printed theorem assumes only
Assumptions 4–5; with `µ ≡ 0` (so `Z ≡ 1`, `P^M = P`) Assumptions 4–5 hold, `Z^M_0 = 1` and
`λ_m` divides by zero, so the non-degeneracy `P^M ≠ P` is added.
`NegPartL2` (`(C_T)^− ∈ L²(P)`) is the finite-variance presupposition of problem (14). -/
theorem theorem_1_i (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hL2 : M.NegPartL2) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (hne : ¬ (M.Z p.T =ᵐ[M.P] fun _ => (1 : ℝ))) (m P R : ℝ)
    (ξ δ χξ L : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (hξ : IsStronglyProgressive M.F ξ)
    (hδ : IsStronglyProgressive M.F δ) (hχξ : M.IsXGains ξ χξ) (hL : M.IsBtIntegral δ L)
    (hrepV : ∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T → M.V P R t =ᵐ[M.P] fun ω => M.V0 P R + χξ t ω + L t ω)
    (ζ χζ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (hζ : IsStronglyProgressive M.G ζ) (hχζ : M.IsXGains ζ χζ)
    (hrepZ : ∀ t : ℝ≥0, t ≤ p.T → M.ZM t =ᵐ[M.P] fun ω => M.ZM0 + χζ t ω) :
    ∃ θ χ : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ, (θ, χ) ∈ M.AX ∧
      (∀ᵐ u ∂(volume.restrict (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) (p.T : ℝ))), ∀ᵐ ω ∂M.P,
        θ u.toNNReal ω = -ξ u.toNNReal ω - ζ u.toNNReal ω / M.ZM u.toNNReal ω *
          ((m * M.ZM0 - M.V0 P R) / (M.ZM0 - 1) - M.V P R u.toNNReal ω - χ u.toNNReal ω)) ∧
      ∫ ω, (M.H P R ω + χ p.T ω) ∂M.P = m ∧
      evariance (fun ω => M.H P R ω + χ p.T ω) M.P = M.Bval m P R ∧
      ∀ θ' χ' : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ, (θ', χ') ∈ M.AX → ∫ ω, (M.H P R ω + χ' p.T ω) ∂M.P = m →
        evariance (fun ω => M.H P R ω + χ' p.T ω) M.P = M.Bval m P R →
        χ' p.T =ᵐ[M.P] χ p.T := by sorry

end HedgePSNV
